@startuml usage1_two_inputs
' Where a GOPAL policy's inputs come from.
'
' The commonest misunderstanding about a policy library is that it evaluates
' "your model". It does not. It evaluates two kinds of statement that come from
' two different places and carry two different levels of proof, and nearly
' every question about adopting GOPAL turns out to be about one of them.
!include gopal-style.puml

title How a GOPAL policy gets its inputs

rectangle "**Your AI system**\nmodel, prompts, retrieval,\napplication code" as SYS #line:94A3B8;back:FFFFFF

rectangle "**Declared facts**\n//asserted by a person//\n\nCE marking affixed\nLogs retained six months\nDeployer informed workers" as DECL #line:0F766E;back:E6F4F1

rectangle "**Evaluators**\nAICertify running DeepEval,\nLangFair, or one you write" as EVAL #line:6D4AA2;back:FFFFFF

rectangle "**Measured metrics**\n//computed by a tool//\n\nToxicity score\nFairness disparity\nContent-safety score" as MEAS #line:6D4AA2;back:F1EBFA

rectangle "**GOPAL policy**\nRego, cited to the article\nit enforces" as POL #line:4F46E5;back:EEF2FF

rectangle "**Verdict**\nsatisfied · not satisfied\n· no conclusion" as VERD #line:4338CA;back:FFFFFF

SYS -[#0F766E]-> DECL : a person asserts
SYS -[#6D4AA2]-> EVAL : is evaluated by
EVAL -[#6D4AA2]-> MEAS : produces
DECL -[#0F766E]-> POL
MEAS -[#6D4AA2]-> POL
POL -[#4338CA]-> VERD

note bottom of DECL
  No tool can measure these. They are facts
  about the organisation, not the model, which
  is why a complete model card answers only
  5 of the 184 inputs the policies read.
end note

note bottom of VERD
  **No conclusion is not a pass.** A policy
  given nothing says so, rather than
  defaulting to compliant.
end note

@enduml
